const description = "How to add and subtract fractions with different denominators, find a common denominator, and simplify the answer.";
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/how-to-add-and-subtract-fractions.html">
  <aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
  <div class="wrap article">
    <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Maths basics</p>
    <h1>How to Add and Subtract Fractions</h1>
    <p class="lead">Fractions with the same denominator are easy to add, but most sums have different denominators. A common denominator makes the pieces the same size, so they can be added.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>Same denominator</h2>
<p>Add the numerators and keep the denominator. For example, 2/7 + 3/7 = 5/7.</p>
<h2>Different denominators</h2>
<p>Find a number both denominators divide into, such as their product. For 1/2 + 1/3, multiply the denominators to get 6. Rewrite 1/2 as 3/6 and 1/3 as 2/6, then add: 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6.</p>
<h2>Simplifying</h2>
<p>Divide the numerator and denominator by their greatest common divisor. 10/12 becomes 5/6 when both are divided by 2.</p>
<h2>Subtraction</h2>
<p>Use the same method, but subtract the numerators. 3/4 minus 1/6 becomes 9/12 minus 2/12, which is 7/12.</p>
<h2>Try it yourself</h2>
<p>The <a href="/tools/fraction-calculator.html">Fraction Calculator</a> handles addition, subtraction, multiplication and division, and shows the answer in lowest terms.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>To add 1/2 and 1/3, the common denominator is 6. Write 1/2 as 3/6 and 1/3 as 2/6, then add: 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6. Since 5 and 6 share no common factor, 5/6 is in lowest terms.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>Why do I need a common denominator?</summary><p>You can only add pieces that are the same size. The denominator sets the size of each piece, so both fractions must use the same size before you add.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How do I simplify a fraction?</summary><p>Divide the top and bottom by their greatest common factor. For 10/12, the factor is 2, so the answer is 5/6.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What is an improper fraction?</summary><p>One where the numerator is bigger than the denominator, such as 7/4. It equals a mixed number, 1 3/4.</p></details>
      </section>
